  "final_message": "Your hunch is right, and the effect is even more concentrated than \"a few ports\" suggests. Three ports account for the whole dip.\n\n**The dip is only in 2024 Q2.** 2023 Q2 was normal at 13,036 TEU. 2024 Q2 dropped to 9,622 TEU, compared with an average of about 13,070 for the other seven quarters. That's a shortfall of about 3,450 TEU (about 26%). Shipment counts and dollar value fell by similar amounts.\n\n**Brackwater, Fennick and Ivelholm nearly stopped shipping that quarter:**\n\n| Port | Usual quarter (TEU) | 2024 Q2 (TEU) | Shipments, 2024 Q2 vs usual |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Brackwater | ~1,820 | 466 | 7 vs ~20 |\n| Fennick | ~1,670 | 366 | 4 vs ~19 |\n| Ivelholm | ~1,610 | 375 | 4 vs ~17 |\n\n- Those three are about 3,900 TEU below their usual level, which is more than the entire dip. The other seven ports together were slightly *above* their average (Cindervale and Juniper Reach were up the most).\n- The timing is sharp. Together the three ports shipped 22 times in March 2024, then 5 times in each of April, May and June, then 21 times in July. It looks like a three-month outage or a gap in the data, not a gradual slowdown.\n- By carrier type, the drop is mostly container traffic, which is the main cargo at these ports.\n\nBecause the drop starts and ends so abruptly, it's worth checking whether those ports really shipped less, or whether some of their data was lost for that period. A real slowdown would usually show up more gradually.\n\nThis was a read-only analysis and I didn't change `notebooks/explore.py`. I can add cells with the quarterly table by port and a monthly chart if you'd like them in the notebook.",
